    </div>
    <Fragment set:html={`<h2>The Correct Dead Hang Position</h2>
        <p>A correct dead hang uses fully extended arms, a firm grip and a still body. Straighten your elbows fully without forcing them past straight. Your bodyweight hangs straight down from the bar through your skeleton. Muscles work only to maintain grip and alignment.</p>
        <p>Shoulder position depends on your goal. Relaxed shoulders make a <a href="/passive-vs-active-hang/">passive hang</a>. It stretches the shoulders and trunk. Engaged shoulder blades make an active hang. It trains shoulder stability. Both need straight arms.</p>
        <p>Your core stays lightly engaged throughout the hold. Use just enough tension to prevent swinging and not so much that it restricts your breathing. Your legs hang straight down with feet together.</p>
        <p>The sections below break down each body part from hands to head.</p>

        <h2>Hand and Grip Position</h2>
        <p>Wrap all four fingers over the top of the bar and place your thumbs underneath, wrapping toward your fingers. This full grip is a secure option. A thumbless (false) grip raises the chance of slipping.</p>
        <p>Space your hands at shoulder width. This spacing opens the shoulders and shares the load across both forearms.</p>
        <p>Most pull-up bars are 28 to 32 mm across; thicker bars are harder to hold. A bar in that range lets most hands wrap fully.</p>
        <p>Sweaty hands often shorten a hold. Chalk dries the skin and increases friction between skin and bar. Use it if sweat and not fatigue ends your hang. No study of chalk and dead hang time was found. See the <a href="/best-chalk/">chalk guide</a> for the options.</p>

        <h2>Shoulder Position: Passive vs Active</h2>
        <p>Shoulder position is the main choice in a dead hang. Passive and active hangs both use straight arms but load the shoulders differently.</p>

        <h3>Passive Shoulder Position</h3>
        <p>Let your shoulders rise toward your ears and do not fight gravity. In a passive hang your bodyweight stretches the upper trapezius and opens the shoulder toward its full overhead range.</p>
        <p>Use the passive position for shoulder and trunk stretching. Orthopedic surgeon John M. Kirsch proposed overhead hanging for shoulder impingement in a self-published book (5th ed. 2019). No published randomized trial has tested it (Europe PMC search of October 2026). Ask a clinician before hanging with shoulder pain. Read the <a href="/shoulder-health/">shoulder health guide</a> for more. Holds of 20 to 30 seconds per set are common training practice and not a tested prescription.</p>

        <h3>Active Shoulder Position</h3>
        <p>Pull your shoulder blades down and together while keeping your arms straight. In an active hang your shoulders drop a few centimeters away from your ears. Your lats and lower trapezius work to hold the shoulder blades in place.</p>
        <p>Use the active position for shoulder blade control and pull-up preparation. An active hang practices the shoulder blade position used at the start of a pull-up. Holds of 15 to 20 seconds per set are common training practice and not a tested prescription.</p>

        <h2>Core and Lower Body</h2>
        <p>Tuck your pelvis slightly by tilting your hip bones upward. This flattens your lower back and limits excessive arching. Think of pulling your belt buckle toward your ribcage without crunching.</p>
        <p>Draw your ribcage down toward your pelvis. This stops the chest from flaring outward. Flared ribs suggest your core has disengaged. Keeping the ribs and pelvis connected holds your spine in a neutral hanging position.</p>
        <p>Squeeze your glutes lightly. Gluteal activation steadies your pelvis and limits lower body sway. You should feel a gentle contraction and not a maximum squeeze. This cue can reduce swinging.</p>
        <p>Bring your legs together with toes pointed downward. Separated legs invite rotation. Bent knees shift your center of mass and start pendulum swinging. Straight legs with pointed toes make one clean line from bar to feet.</p>

        <h2>Head and Neck</h2>
        <p>Tuck your chin slightly to keep a neutral neck. Imagine making a double chin. This position aligns your head with your spine and avoids neck strain.</p>
        <p>Direct your gaze forward or slightly downward. Looking up extends the neck. Looking straight down rounds the upper back. A forward gaze keeps the neck and spine in line.</p>
        <p>Relax your jaw. Clenching your teeth can add tension in the neck. A relaxed jaw also helps your breathing rhythm.</p>

        <h2>Form Checklist</h2>
        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Dead Hang Position Cues</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Full grip with thumbs wrapped around the bar.</li>
            <li>Hands at shoulder width on a bar about 28 to 32 mm across.</li>
            <li>Arms fully extended with elbows straight but not forced past straight.</li>
            <li>Shoulders relaxed (passive) or shoulder blades down (active).</li>
            <li>Pelvis tucked with hip bones tilted upward.</li>
            <li>Ribcage drawn down toward pelvis.</li>
            <li>Glutes lightly engaged for pelvic stability.</li>
            <li>Legs together with toes pointed downward.</li>
            <li>Chin tucked for a neutral neck.</li>
            <li>Gaze forward or slightly down.</li>
            <li>Jaw relaxed and unclenched.</li>
            <li>Breathing slowly and not holding your breath.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <p>Run through this checklist every time you set up. With practice these cues become easier to hit.</p>

        <h2>What Bad Form Looks Like</h2>

        <h3>Bent Arms</h3>
        <p>Elbows flexed at any angle turn the dead hang into a flexed-arm hang. The biceps take over some of the work from the forearms. Your hold tends to end sooner. Straighten your elbows fully without forcing them past straight.</p>

        <h3>Shrugged Shoulders</h3>
        <p>Actively shrugging differs from passive shoulder elevation. Passive elevation is correct in a relaxed hang. Forced upward shrugging contracts the upper trapezius and works against the relaxed stretch. Let gravity do the work.</p>

        <h3>Swinging</h3>
        <p>Body sway in any direction wastes energy and shortens your hold. Swinging suggests too little core engagement or an uncontrolled dismount from the previous set. Start each hang from a dead stop. Engage your glutes and core before fully releasing your weight.</p>

        <h3>Hyperextended Spine</h3>
        <p>An arched lower back can make the hold uncomfortable. The pelvis tilts forward and the ribcage flares. Tuck your pelvis and draw your ribs down to correct it.</p>

        <p>Fix form errors early and before you add time to your hold. The <a href="/deadhang-mistakes/">common dead hang mistakes guide</a> covers more errors and their corrections.</p>

